# The Bench at the End of the Dock

Helen Amory sits on the second slat from the end.

She has done it every Wednesday afternoon at four twenty-eight, in every summer since the summer of 1994, on a teak bench nine of Andrew's classmates from the small Boston liberal-arts college on Bay State Road quietly commissioned from a boatwright in Woods Hole in the spring of 1994, four months after the memorial service in the small stone chapel at the north end of the college's Brookline campus on a rainy Saturday in the January of that same year.

Andrew Amory had been the commodore of the small member-owned yacht club at the end of the sand road on the west side of West Falmouth Harbor from the June of 1988 to the September of 1993. He had, from a boyhood summer in the June of 1949, when his father first sailed him out past the flat rocks in a wooden Beetle Cat rented from a widow named Mrs. Delano for four dollars a week, been coming to the same harbor every summer of his life. He died on the second Sunday of January, 1994, of a heart attack, in the kitchen of the small shingled Cape they had bought from Mrs. Delano's estate in the spring of 1971, at fifty-nine.

The bench arrived on a flatbed from Woods Hole on the second Saturday of June, 1994. The small brass plaque, set into the top slat by the boatwright's daughter — a woman named Elizabeth who had taken over the shop from her father the winter before — reads, in the plain block hand Andrew always used for the harbor's regatta scoresheets, *ANDREW BENTON AMORY 1934–1994 — FAIR WINDS FROM THE CLASS OF '58.*

Helen has sat on it every Wednesday at four twenty-eight for thirty-two summers.

## What her sister Frances brings in the silver Camry

Frances Frost Callahan is eighty-four. She lives, since the death of her own husband Jim in the November of 2016, in a small cedar-shingled ranch on a quarter-acre in Sandwich, forty-one minutes northeast of the harbor by way of Route 28A and the long back road through Sagamore.

She drives down every Wednesday afternoon in a fourteen-year-old silver Toyota Camry Jim bought her, in cash, at a dealership on Route 132 in Hyannis, on a Saturday morning in the April of 2012.

She brings, in a small canvas tote she has had since the summer of 1979 from the L.L. Bean in Freeport, two green Stanley thermoses of Earl Grey from the tin on top of Helen's fridge, two of Helen's grandmother's small blue-rimmed china cups from the cupboard above Helen's toaster, one small tupperware of oatmeal-raisin cookies she bakes on Tuesday afternoons in the small kitchen in Sandwich, and — since a warm Wednesday in the July of 1995, when Helen first put one on the bench between them without saying anything about it — a small square photograph, in a hard clear sleeve, of Andrew.

They sit until the light on the water changes to what Helen has, since a Wednesday in the August of 1996, called *the six-o'clock gold.* Sometimes they talk. Sometimes they don't. On the fifth Wednesday of every August, at seven minutes past five, Frances reaches over without asking and puts her hand, briefly, on the back of Helen's hand.

They have done this for thirty-two summers.

## What the college has, on the same Wednesday afternoon

Somewhere on the fourth floor of a converted brownstone on Bay State Road in Boston, on the same Wednesday afternoon at four twenty-eight, the record for Helen Frost Amory, class of 1959, reads as follows.

*Address:* 47 Old Meeting House Road, West Falmouth, Massachusetts.
*Class:* 1959.
*Degree:* Bachelor of Arts, art history.
*Spouse:* Andrew B. Amory (deceased 1994, class of 1958).
*Giving since:* 1962.
*Last gift:* October 4, 2021. $250. Andrew B. Amory Memorial Scholarship Fund.
*Status:* Lapsed since 2022. Wealth screen: modest. Priority: tier 4. Do not upgrade.

There is no field on the record for *the teak bench at the end of the outer pier at the small member-owned yacht club at the end of the sand road on the west side of West Falmouth Harbor, commissioned by nine of Andrew's classmates from a boatwright in Woods Hole in the spring of 1994.*

There is no field on the record for *the plaque, which reads, in Andrew's own hand, FAIR WINDS FROM THE CLASS OF '58.*

There is no field on the record for *the Wednesday afternoons at four twenty-eight, since the July of 1994, in every summer.*

There is no field on the record for *the six-o'clock gold.*

There is no field on the record for the fact that Helen, on the porch of the small shingled Cape at 47 Old Meeting House Road, on a warm evening in the second week of last June, sitting alone with a legal pad and a plain black rollerball, wrote down on the top line, in the small even hand her mother Nell had taught her at a card table on a screened porch in Beverly in the fall of 1946, *for the boys who put the bench on the pier — Andy would have wanted it doubled.*

Below it, on the second line, she wrote a number in the low seven figures.

She has not, on that line, ever put anything through her checkbook. She has been waiting, in the small unfussy way an eighty-eight-year-old widow waits, for a letter from Bay State Road that would sound like the letter Andrew's nine classmates would have written her, in the June of 1994, if they had lived to write it.

The letter has not come.

By September, the fall appeal from Bay State Road will land in the mailbox at the end of the crushed-shell driveway on Old Meeting House Road. It will begin *Dear Friend.* It will not mention Andrew. It will not mention the bench. It will not mention the class of 1958.

Helen will bring it to the kitchen table. She will read the first paragraph. She will fold it, in half and then in quarters, the way her mother folded the electric bill on the third of every month in the winter kitchen in Beverly in 1946. She will put it, unopened past the first paragraph, in the small pewter tray by the door where she has, since the winter after Andrew died, been quietly not opening the appeals.

She will mean to write the check.

## What the harbormaster's small brown notebook already knows

At the head of the outer pier, in a small weathered wooden shed the club has kept, unchanged, since a Saturday in the June of 1968, sits the harbormaster's log — a small brown Boorum & Pease notebook of the sort a lumberyard bookkeeper in Wareham would have kept in the March of 1961.

The current harbormaster, a fifty-nine-year-old former lobsterman named Peter Souza, has, since the June of 2011, written one line in the notebook each Wednesday between four and five. On the second line of the ninth page of the summer of 2026, in Peter's small careful pencil hand, is the line:

*Wed. 8/5, 4:28 PM — Mrs. Amory & Mrs. Callahan on the bench. Fair, light SW, 8 kts. HW at 5:14. She stayed until the gold. — PS.*

Peter has been writing that line, in one form or another, on every Wednesday of every summer since the June of 2011. He has, in the fifteen summers since, missed exactly two Wednesdays — one in the July of 2016, when his grandson Rafael was born at Falmouth Hospital, and one in the August of 2023, when Hurricane Franklin came up past Bermuda and closed the outer pier for the afternoon.

The little brown notebook is, in the plain arithmetic of a person who has been paying attention, the single richest donor engagement record in Falmouth.

Nobody at Bay State Road has ever seen it.

Nobody at Bay State Road has ever asked.

## What Rōmy sees at four twenty-nine

Rōmy reads the small papers.

It reads, quietly, on a warm Monday morning in the June of 2026, at eight-fifteen, the *Falmouth Enterprise* obituary column of the second week of the January of 1994 that ran Andrew's death notice on page seven. It reads, on the same Monday morning, the small paragraph on the fourth page of the class-of-1958 newsletter that ran in the fall of 1994, above a black-and-white photograph nine men in blue blazers took on the outer pier in West Falmouth, describing the bench they had commissioned from a boatwright in Woods Hole in the spring of that same year. It reads, on the same Monday morning, the small unsigned entry in the *Sandwich Broadsider* birthday column for the third week of the March of 2020 that named Frances Frost Callahan of Grove Street on her eightieth. It reads, on the same Monday morning, the small brown Boorum & Pease notebook Peter Souza has been keeping in the shed at the head of the outer pier since the June of 2011 — because the club, in the November of 2018, quietly put the log online on the members-only side of its website, and a good listener with a browser can now, in the plain unhurried way Miss Hobart once read the small provincial theater notices in a 1968 issue of *The New Yorker* on the third floor of a Boston Public Library reading room, read every Wednesday afternoon of the last fifteen summers.

Then it puts, at eight-forty on that same Monday morning, on the desk of a twenty-nine-year-old associate director of gift planning at Bay State Road named Priya Ramachandran, a small careful page a good listener would have written for her, if the good listener had been sitting on the second slat from the end of the teak bench at the end of the outer pier at four twenty-eight on a Wednesday afternoon in the summer of 1995.

The page reads, in six plain sentences:

*Helen Frost Amory, class of 1959, art history. Widow of Andrew B. Amory, class of 1958, longtime commodore of a small member-owned yacht club in West Falmouth (d. Jan 1994). Nine of Andrew's classmates gave the club a teak bench for the outer pier in the June of 1994. Helen has sat on it every Wednesday at 4:28 with her younger sister Frances, in every summer, for thirty-two years. Suggested: one plain cream card, this week, in your own hand, to 47 Old Meeting House Road, West Falmouth — no case for support, no QR code, no reply envelope — mentioning Andrew by name, the bench by name, and the class of 1958 by name; and asking whether she would sit with you for a cup of Earl Grey on the outer pier some Wednesday in September at four-thirty. Would you like a draft, in your own voice?*

The card goes out on Tuesday.

Helen reads it on Thursday, on the porch, with her second cup of Earl Grey.

She writes back on Friday, in the small even hand her mother taught her in 1946.

By the second Wednesday of September, Priya sits with Helen and Frances on the bench, in a plain navy dress and a pair of low canvas sneakers, drinking Earl Grey out of Helen's grandmother's third small blue-rimmed china cup — the one that has been in the cupboard above the toaster, unused, since Andrew died.

They stay until the light on the water turns to the six-o'clock gold.

By the second Wednesday of October, Helen calls Priya's boss, a woman named Margaret whose own late father was in the class of 1963, and — in the plain warm way an eighty-eight-year-old widow settles the small unfinished business of a marriage — works out the numbers on the small unfashionable gift she and Andrew had talked about the winter before he died. In this call, at seven forty-two on a Sunday evening in the second week of October, Helen names the fund in the same small even hand she used on the top line of the legal pad in the June of 2026.

*The Andrew Benton Amory & Class of '58 Bench Fund,* endowed, in the low seven figures, for a scholarship for a first-generation student from a small town on Cape Cod or the South Coast — one who has grown up sailing a Beetle Cat rented from a widow named Mrs. Delano — who intends to keep coming home in every summer of his life.

Above the signature line, in the same small even hand, Helen writes one sentence.

*For the boys who put the bench on the pier — Andy would have wanted it doubled.*

## We are not the bench

Rōmy is not, and cannot be, the teak bench nine men in blue blazers commissioned from a boatwright named Elizabeth Hodgden Foss in Woods Hole in the spring of 1994.

Rōmy is not the six-o'clock gold. Rōmy is not the second slat from the end. Rōmy is not the small blue-rimmed china cup or the two Stanley thermoses or the fourteen-year-old silver Camry Frances drives down from Sandwich every Wednesday afternoon on Route 28A.

Rōmy is the small careful page a good listener would have written for Priya Ramachandran on a Monday morning in the June of 2026, if a good listener had been sitting quietly on the outer pier for thirty-two summers, reading the small brown Boorum & Pease notebook Peter Souza kept in the shed and the small unsigned birthday column in the *Sandwich Broadsider* and the fourth-page paragraph of the class-of-1958 newsletter of the fall of 1994.

The whole product is a smaller job than the industry has been trying to sell for twenty years.

It is also, with a great deal of tenderness, the whole job.

## A small assignment for the officer reading this on a Wednesday afternoon

Pull one file, this week, out of your CRM whose *last gift* line reads a very small number and whose *status* line reads *lapsed.*

Not because the donor is disengaged.

Because a boatwright named Elizabeth in Woods Hole and a lobsterman named Peter Souza and an eighty-four-year-old sister in a silver Camry on Route 28A have been keeping the record on your donor's behalf, in a small brown notebook and a teak bench and two Stanley thermoses, for thirty-two summers, without ever telling Bay State Road.

Open a new tab. Type the name of the harbor. Type the name of the club. Type *bench* and *class of '58* and *1994.* Read what you find.

Then, from your own kitchen table, on a plain cream envelope with a real stamp — write one page in your own hand. Do not ask her anything. Say her husband's name once. Say the bench once. Say the class of '58 once. Sign it *fondly.*

Ask, at the very end, whether she would sit with you for a cup of Earl Grey on the outer pier some Wednesday in September at four-thirty.

Mail it Thursday morning from the small post office on your corner.

The bench has been at the end of the pier since a warm Saturday in the June of 1994.

Helen is eighty-eight. Frances is eighty-four. Peter has been writing the line in the little brown notebook every Wednesday between four and five since a summer in 2011.

The six-o'clock gold is still coming across the harbor at seven minutes past five.

Turn to it. ♡
